Simple soft tissue contusion from dropped heavy object to dorsum of foot
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Dorsal foot ecchymosis, Dropped object mechanism, Preserved weight-bearing ability

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-024
Reason for Visit: Acute severe foot pain and swelling after dropping a heavy object, requiring assessment to rule out metatarsal fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a heavy blunt force crush mechanism to the dorsal foot, resulting in pain and swelling. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for acute compartment syndrome of the foot, clear major midfoot/metatarsal fractures, and establish safe weight-bearing parameters prior to daytime radiography availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I dropped a heavy book on the top of my foot; it is throbbing constantly and turning purple.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a heavy, blunt object fell directly onto the unprotected top of the foot 3 hours ago. This resulted in a localized, dull throbbing ache and point tenderness over the dorsal midfoot. The patient notes the gradual appearance of purplish/blue ecchymosis and development of mild to moderate localized soft tissue swelling obscuring normal extensor tendon contours. The patient has a preserved ability to bear weight and walk, albeit with a mildly antalgic gait favoring the heel. The pain is primarily superficial and poorly localized.
Functional Status: Ambulating independently but cautiously.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor impedance on stairs due to pain with forefoot loading.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, sitting with the foot resting flat on the floor.
Physical Exam: Foot examination reveals dorsal foot ecchymosis and mild to moderate localized soft tissue swelling. There is a complete absence of sharp, localized bony point tenderness over the metatarsal shafts or base of the 5th metatarsal. Active and passive range of motion of the toes and ankle joint remains intact. The patient displays a preserved weight-bearing ability, taking 4 steps independently. Strong, universally palpable dorsalis pedis pulse is noted. Normal sensation over the deep peroneal nerve distribution within the first dorsal web space.
Environmental Observations: Heavy book returned to shelf; no ongoing hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: Took oral ibuprofen 1 hour prior to arrival.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple soft tissue contusion to dorsum of foot - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Metatarsal fracture - Ruled out due to: Ability to comfortably bear weight, absence of severe focal bony pinpoint tenderness, and diffuse rather than localized swelling.

Lisfranc (midfoot) injury - Ruled out due to: Intact plantar arch, absence of severe midfoot pain with passive pronation/abduction, and no plantar ecchymosis.

Acute compartment syndrome of the foot - Ruled out due to: Soft, compressible compartments, intact distal pulses, normal two-point discrimination, and absence of pain out of proportion with passive toe stretch.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The mechanism of a heavy object dropped onto the dorsal foot inherently carries a high index of suspicion for underlying metatarsal fractures or midfoot structural disruption. On-site bedside assessment confidently localized the pathology to superficial soft tissue contusion, demonstrating a preserved ability to bear weight and an absence of focal bony tenderness. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear the patient of surgical emergencies (Lisfranc injury, compartment syndrome), thereby safely bypassing an immediate emergency department presentation and instigating an acute RICE protocol overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated RICE management (Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation). Prescribed scheduled paracetamol and NSAIDs for the acute inflammatory phase. Plain anteroposterior, lateral, and oblique foot radiographs are clinically indicated tomorrow if pain worsens or weight-bearing becomes impossible, to definitively rule out occult fractures.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply ice packs for 15 minutes every 2 hours and elevate the foot above heart level. This harnesses gravity and vasoconstriction to rapidly minimize blood pooling in the tissues, directly reducing throbbing pain and accelerating recovery.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days if symptoms do not significantly improve, to arrange delayed radiography.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the foot becomes rock-hard, agonizingly painful to touch, pale, cold, or if numbness develops between the first and second toes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Swelling can sometimes mask small fractures; if weight-bearing becomes impossible, an x-ray is necessary.
Clinical Course: The bruising will spread and deepen in color over the next 3 days due to gravity. The swelling should peak at 48 hours and resolve over 1-2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you become completely unable to put any weight on the foot, or if the pain suddenly worsens dramatically despite painkillers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A contusion is a severe bruise where small blood vessels are crushed, bleeding into the surrounding tissue. Elevation and ice stop this bleeding and reduce the pressure buildup.
Actionable Steps: Sleep with your foot elevated on two pillows. Wear a supportive, stiff-soled shoe when walking. Avoid standing completely still for long periods.

